.TH READDIR_R 3
.SH NAME
readdir_r \- read a directory entry into a caller-supplied buffer
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <dirent.h>
.PP
.BI "int readdir_r(DIR *" dirp ", struct dirent *" entry ", struct dirent **" result ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B readdir_r()
function reads the next directory entry from the directory stream
.IR dirp ,
storing it in the caller-supplied buffer pointed to by
.IR entry
and setting
.I *result
to point at that buffer.
At end-of-directory
.I *result
is set to NULL.
.PP
Unlike
.BR readdir (3),
which returns a pointer to storage that may be overwritten by a later call,
.B readdir_r()
places the entry in storage owned by the caller, so the returned entry is not
clobbered by subsequent activity on other directory streams.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success
.B readdir_r()
returns 0; on end-of-directory it returns 0 with
.I *result
set to NULL.
On error it returns a positive error number.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.IR dirp ", " entry ", or " result
is NULL.
.TP
.B EBADF
.I dirp
does not refer to an open directory stream.
.SH NOTES
.B readdir_r()
is marked obsolescent in POSIX.1-2008; new code should prefer
.BR readdir (3),
which on this system already returns a pointer into per-stream storage.
.B readdir_r()
is provided for portability with code \(em such as the X11
.I <X11/Xos_r.h>
multithread-safe path \(em that calls it when
.B _POSIX_THREAD_SAFE_FUNCTIONS
is defined.
.PP
This implementation serializes against the shared per-stream read buffer rather
than supporting concurrent reads of the same
.IR dirp ;
the guarantee it provides is that the returned
.I entry
is caller-owned.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR readdir (3),
.BR opendir (3),
.BR closedir (3)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2008 (obsolescent).
